INFO:agent.loop:\u26a1 Executing tool: 'knowledge_agent' with arguments: {"source_title": "Conti public attribution, leaks, and DOJ case record", "source_url": "SRC-001; SRC-002; SRC-003; SRC-004", "text": "Benchmark evidence: Rewards for Justice identifies Conti also as Wizard Spider, assesses it as a Russian government-linked ransomware-as-a-service group targeting U.S. and Western critical infrastructure. It says aliases Target, Reshaev, Professor, Tramp, and Dandis are believed linked to Conti; associated location Russia; associated applications include Conti RaaS, TrickBot, IcedID, Cobalt Strike, and ZLoader. It describes more than 1,000 operations, more than 400 organizations worldwide victimized and over 290 in the U.S., with theft plus encryption and possible publication/sale of stolen data. Rapid7 reports the Feb. 27, 2022 ContiLeaks disclosure of 393 JSON files/14 MB of chats, some encrypted, containing internal communications, tools, exploit/design discussions, credentials and alleged infrastructure details; it assesses the leaker as likely a senior member or coalition of affiliates. Rapid7 describes hiring/interviews, coding exercises, salary negotiations, product development, internal operators, phishing, testing against endpoint detection, and says Conti remained active and significant after infrastructure takedown. CIS describes Conti as a notorious double-extortion RaaS, with affiliates sharing profits, and reports leak-based descriptions of business units: Coders, Testers, Administrators, Reverse Engineers, Penetration Testers/Hackers, with spamming contracted externally; more than 60 personnel were reportedly employed in July 2021. CIS also reports alleged ties to TrickBot, later dismantling/rebranding into smaller units, and cites MS-ISAC reports of 22 Conti-related SLTT attacks from mid-2020 through Jan 2022. DOJ record lists cases 3:23-CR-00092 against Maksim Galochkin, Maksim Rudenskiy, Mikhail Tsarev, Andrey Zhuykov, and 3:23-CR-00088 against Oleksii Lytvynenko. DOJ says the defendants were charged in relation to a Conti ransomware conspiracy; the four named in 2023 were charged with conspiracy to violate CFAA and wire fraud, and Lytvynenko was charged with conspiracy to deploy Conti; DOJ states defendants presumed innocent. DOJ page reports Lytvynenko pleaded guilty to Count 2 conspiracy to commit wire fraud on June 10, 2026, with sentencing scheduled Sept. 10, 2026. No benchmark source establishes a specific alias-to-defendant identity mapping."}
